course: everyday
lesson: 293
title: Being kind to yourself, money, screens and fair play
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Growing up means looking after yourself in small ways: your feelings, your money, your time, your privacy and how you play.
explain: Online pictures usually show only people's best moments. Behind every smiling holiday photo there are ordinary and sad days too, because real life has ups and downs for everyone.
explain: Money runs out if you spend it all at once. A girl in Sydney gets $5 a week: she saves $2 in a jar and spends the rest wisely, and by the summer she can buy the kite she wanted.
explain: Screens are fun, but too much time on them can make you grumpy and spoil your sleep. Breaks, outdoor play and limits agreed with your family help.
explain: Every body grows at its own speed, so it is never fair to tease anyone for being taller, shorter or changing sooner or later than others.
fact: Online pictures often show only the best moments; real life has ups and downs for everyone.
fact: With pocket money, save some and spend some wisely; money runs out if you spend it all at once.
fact: Curiosity helps you learn: ask questions and look things up in books or with an adult.
fact: Helping in the kitchen is useful, but ask an adult for help with sharp knives and very hot water.
fact: The bathroom is private: close the door, and others should knock before coming in.
fact: Too much screen time can affect mood and sleep; take breaks, go outside and keep to agreed limits.
fact: Many children your age do not have a phone; every family decides when the time is right.
fact: Cheating spoils a game, and people will not want to play with a cheat; play fairly.
fact: Bodies grow and change at different times; everyone grows at their own speed.
fact: At your age there is no rush to have a boyfriend or girlfriend; many children have none, and you never have to ask someone out just because others push you.
fact: Never send pictures of yourself to someone online who asks for them; block them and tell a trusted adult.
exercise: A girl scrolls through photos of other children's holidays and feels her life is dull. What is true? | Online pictures show only the best moments | Other children are happy all the time | Her life must be much worse and duller than everyone else's | Photos online always show real life
exercise: You get $5 pocket money each week. What is the wise plan? | Save some and spend some wisely | Spend it all on the very first day | Never spend any of it at all | Lend it all to friends
exercise: What happens if you spend all your pocket money at once? | It runs out and you have none left for later | It grows back by the next morning if you leave the purse closed | The shop gives you more for free | Your bank adds the same amount again
exercise: A boy in Colorado wonders why the sky is blue. What is a good thing to do? | Ask questions and look it up with an adult or in a book | Decide nobody can ever know | Make up an answer and tell everyone in class that it is true | Stop wondering about such things
exercise: Why is it good to be curious? | Curiosity helps you learn | Curiosity makes adults angry | Curious people never need school | Curiosity is only for scientists
exercise: You are helping wash up and see a big kitchen knife and a pot of very hot water. What do you do? | Ask an adult to help with the knife and hot water | Grab the knife quickly out of the bubbles so the job is done fast | Pour the hot water out by yourself | Leave all the washing-up to someone else
exercise: Your brother walks into the bathroom while you are on the toilet. What is the right rule? | Close the door, and others knock before coming in | Bathrooms are for everyone at once | Only adults need privacy | Leave the door open wide so that people know the bathroom is busy
exercise: After three hours of games, a girl feels grumpy and cannot sleep. What helps? | Take breaks, go outside and keep to agreed limits | Play late into the night to relax, then sleep in the next day | Play faster to finish sooner | Watch videos in bed until tired
exercise: You feel left out because you don't have a phone like some classmates. What is true? | Many children your age don't have one; each family decides | Every child your age has a phone | Your parents must buy one at once | Children without phones cannot have any real friends at all at school
exercise: You are losing a board game and could secretly move your piece. What should you do? | Play fairly | Cheat a little when no one is looking | Knock the board over so nobody wins | Say the rules are different at your home
exercise: Why is cheating in games a bad idea? | It spoils the game and people won't want to play with you | It always makes the game more exciting for every player at the table | It is fine if you are losing | Nobody ever notices cheating
exercise: A boy is teased for being the shortest in his class. What is true? | Everyone grows at their own speed | Short children stop growing early | He must have done something wrong | Only tall children grow up healthy
test: A boy sees an influencer's perfect bedroom online and feels bad about his own. What should he remember? | Online pictures show the best bits, and real life has ups and downs | Everyone else's rooms really are perfect, and only his is a mess | He should buy everything she has | Messy rooms only happen in his house
test: A girl wants a $30 toy and gets $5 a week. What is the best plan? | Save part of it each week until she has enough | Spend it all each week and just hope the toy turns up | Borrow $30 from a classmate | Ask for the toy every day
test: Your little sister asks why the Moon changes shape and you don't know. What is a good answer? | «Let's look it up together» | «Nobody knows, so stop asking» | «Questions like that are silly» | «I'll make something up for you»
test: Which kitchen job is fine for a child to do alone? | Drying plastic cups and plates | Washing the sharp carving knife | Draining a pan of boiling pasta | Pouring boiling water into a sink
test: A friend at a sleepover opens the bathroom door without knocking. What is the polite rule? | Knock, because bathroom time is private | Just walk in if the light is on | Shout through the door instead | Wait outside for a moment and then open it anyway
test: Which is TRUE about a lot of screen time? | It can affect mood and sleep | It helps you sleep more deeply | It makes everyone feel happier | It has no effect on how you feel
test: A boy in Ireland is the only one in his group without a phone. Which is TRUE? | Families choose different times for a phone, and that is fine | His parents must not love him | He will never be allowed a phone | He should take a friend's phone home to use at night
test: Your friend keeps winning card games by peeking at your cards. What is likely to happen? | People will stop wanting to play with him | Everyone will want to play with him even more | He will become a famous player | The game will get fairer
test: A girl started growing faster than her friends and gets teased. Which is TRUE? | Bodies change at different times for everyone | She is growing the wrong way | Everyone grows at exactly the same age, so she is ill | Her friends will never grow
test: everyone on insta has a perfect life, mine is boring | Pics show only the best bits, everyone has ups and downs | Yes, other people's lives are always perfect, every single day | You need more followers to be happy | Post more pics to catch up with them
test: got $10 pocket money, spend it all on lollies? | Save some and spend some wisely | Spend it all, money is for spending | Save it all and never spend any | Give it all to your friends
test: im the only one in my class without a phone 😭 | Lots of kids don't, each family decides when | Everyone your age has one by law | Your parents must buy one now | You'll fall behind at school without one in your bag
test: mate peeks at the cards when we play, its fine innit | No, cheating spoils the game and people stop playing | Yes, it's just a bit of fun, everyone peeks a bit | Yes, if he only peeks once | It's fine if he's losing
explain: A girl in Leeds compared herself to perfect pictures online for months and started to feel ugly and sad; her mum helped her take a break, and she felt better.
explain: A boy in Texas spent all his pocket money on sweets the day he got it; when his friends went to the cinema, he had nothing left.
fact: Too much time on screens and social media is linked with worse sleep and lower mood in children.
fact: Money spent in the first day of the week cannot be spent later; that is why people plan.
test: everyone online looks perfect, im so ugly | Pictures show the best bits; talk to someone you trust | They really are perfect, all of the time | You need filters too | Stay online more to look like them
test: just got pocket money, spend it all now? | Save some for later | Spend it all, more comes next week | Lend it all to a friend | Hide it and forget it
test: mate cheats every game, nobody wants to play with him | Cheating spoils games and loses friends | They're just jealous | Cheating is fine if you win | Only cheating at cards counts, other games don't
test: did u hear a girl spent all her birthday money "in one day on sweets" and then "couldn't even buy her mum a present". lesson? | Save some and spend some; money runs out if you spend it all | Spend it fast before it's lost | Sweets are always worth it | Birthday money doesn't need a plan, you can't run out
test: my mate says a boy played games "for 3 days straight without sleeping". what's prob exaggerated | The 3 days; but too much screen time does hurt sleep and mood | That games can be fun for hours | That screens affect sleep | That boys play games
test: kids are saying the girl with no phone is "so poor lol". pass it on? | No, every family decides when the time's right; that's unkind | Yes, she must be poor | Yes, and ask her why in front of everyone at break | Yes, and leave her out of the chat
test: everyone in my class has a boyfriend or girlfriend already and I don't, I'm 10, is something wrong with me? | No, lots of kids don't, and there's no rush at all | Yes, you must get one this week | Yes, you should copy what older kids do | Yes, pretend you have one so nobody laughs
test: classmate: "if you don't ask Mia out you're a loser, everyone's counting on it" — what's right? | You don't have to; do what feels right for you, not what others push | Ask her, because everyone is counting on you and you can't let them down | Pay someone to ask her for you | Tease Mia so they stop asking
test: online someone says "send a pic of u to my bf so he can see if ur cute" — what do you do? | Don't send it, block them and tell a trusted adult | Send one, it's only a face | Send a pic of your friend instead | Ask them to pay you first, then send it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the pictures commonly published online? | They often show only the best moments, while real life has ups and downs | They show life exactly as it is | They are always taken without any editing and show exactly what happened | They show only the worst moments
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a weekly sum of money ought to be planned. | Money spent on the first day cannot be spent later | Money loses its value after one day | Planning doubles the sum available | Money must be spent before the week ends, or it is taken back
exam: It is given that a child assists in the kitchen. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | An adult shall be asked to help with sharp knives and very hot water | The child shall handle all the knives alone, so as to learn quickly | The child shall carry boiling water unaided | Help shall be sought only for washing up
exam: State the consequence of cheating in a game. | It spoils the game, and others will not wish to play | It improves the game for every player and makes it more exciting | It is permitted if the game is long | It has no effect upon friendships
